luation for that round consumes 215ms.\n\nNow examining the trace commit phase, the Poseidon2 row hashing dominates at 231ms across 25 instances, followed by zero hash calculations at 109ms across many instances. The remaining operations like stacking columns and strided compression are much lighter, though there's a merkle tree compression step at 75ms that might actually belong to the openings phase. Overall the GPU reports 442ms of work here, but the wall time is 248ms due to parallelization across threads.\n\nThe core issue is that these hash operations are cryptographic primitives fundamental to the proof system, so optimizing them would require changing the underlying algorithm itself, which isn't feasible. Looking at what's actually addressable, the STARK excluding trace is at 1407ms now—a 1.74x improvement from the baseline of 2455ms—with constraints taking up 983ms of that.\n\nBreaking down the remaining components: LogUp GKR is consuming 522ms, Round 0 takes 290ms, and the MLE rounds another 170ms. Openings account for 175ms split between WHIR at 101ms and Stacked Reduction at 74ms. Trace Commit is 248ms.\n\nThe scaling from APC 0 to APC 300 has only achieved 1.52x reduction (2140ms down to 1407ms), but we need better than 2x to hit the target. That means getting STARK excluding trace down to 1070ms or less at APC 300—requiring another 337ms of cuts. LogUp GKR at 522ms is the biggest opportunity here.\n\nLooking at where the time goes in LogUp GKR: input evaluation dominates at around 520ms on GPU, with the kernel-heavy seg0 being the main bottleneck. The tree building and proving phase uses several fractional sumcheck kernels that total about 232ms. I'm considering two approaches—fusing the many small tree-building kernel launches together to reduce overhead, or running the tree proving across multiple GPU streams instead of sequentially on a single stream.\n\nFor Round 0, which takes 290ms, I'm seeing 557ms of GPU time spread across 8 threads with about 1.9x parallelism. The multi-threaded path kicks in for larger AIRs at APC 300. The main opportunity here is batching the thousand-plus small Round 0 kernels using descriptor arrays like we did for MLE rounds, though increasing beyond 8 threads showed diminishing returns since each batch already contains 20-42 AIRs. I'm seeing a contradiction between the reports—the multistream optimization showed a 1.91x improvement at APC 300, but the prealloc report claims batches of 20-42 AIRs never reach the >=100 threshold needed for multi-threading. Let me trace through the logic: if 623 AIRs are split into memory-limited batches of 20-42, then individually no batch would hit 100, yet the optimization clearly worked. Either the threshold applies differently (maybe per-segment rather than per-batch), or the code changed between when these reports were generated.\n\nRather than getting lost in the implementation details, I should focus on the profiling data itself. Looking at the nsys NVTX traces and kernel summaries will show me where GPU time is actually spent and what the real optimization opportunities are.\n\nBreaking down the APC 300 kernel execution times, the trace generation dominates at 615ms, followed by GKR input evaluation at 457ms and Round 0 constraint checking at 237ms. The trace commit hashing takes another 231ms, with Round 0 interaction evaluation at 215ms. The remaining kernels—hash computation, NTT operations, Merkle tree operations, and GKR round computation—each contribute between 70-110ms.\n\nContinuing through the profile, I see several more GKR-related kernels: the second GKR input evaluation variant at 64ms, bit reversal preprocessing at 50ms, multifold operations at 47ms, and partial precomputation at 45ms. Aggregating these metrics, GKR operations total around 754ms across all kernels, while Round 0 specific work accounts for approximately 564ms of in-scope GPU time.\n\nLooking at the remaining phases, trace commitment kernels consume about 369ms combined, MLE and stacked reduction add roughly 100ms, and the WHIR opening operations span around 293ms. But I'm realizing these kernel times are misleading—many of these run concurrently across different GPU streams, so the actual wall-clock time per phase is significantly lower than summing individual kernel durations. That's the whole benefit of the multi-stream architecture.\n\nSo I should focus on what's actually improvable. One promising angle is batching the Round 0 constraint and interaction kernels together. At APC 300, Round 0 launches nearly 935 kernels just for the parallel coset-evaluation work, and most of them are quite small. I could apply the descriptor-array batching pattern that's already proven effective elsewhere to consolidate these launches.\n\nThe current setup runs Round 0 across 8 threads with separate CUDA streams per thread, where each thread processes a chunk of AIRs and launches kernels serially on its own stream. If I batch kernels within each thread, I'd cut down the per-thread launch overhead. With roughly 39 AIRs per thread and 2-4 kernel launches per AIR, that's 80-160 launches per thread, which at 5 microseconds each adds up to maybe 0.4-0.8ms per thread—probably not the main bottleneck.\n\nBut looking at the actual numbers, the total GPU time is 564ms while wall time is only 290ms. With 8 threads, I'd expect closer to 70ms theoretically, so we're only getting about 1.9x parallelism. That suggests the issue is load imbalance across threads, memory contention from the async allocator, CPU-side overhead between kernels, or GPU SM contention between streams. The prealloc approach failed because the buffers were too large, so I'm considering fusing the constraint and interaction kernels instead.\n\nFor each AIR, Round 0 launches separate kernels for both constraint and interaction evaluation. If I fuse these into a single kernel, I could cut the launch count in half and potentially improve data locality, though the generic templated constraint logic might make this complex.\n\nLooking at the GKR tree proving portion, the compute_round_and_revert kernel dominates at 69ms across 141 instances, followed by bit reversal and partial precomputation kernels. These kernels could potentially be parallelized across multiple streams to hide latency.\n\nThe real bottleneck is the poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes kernel in Trace Commit, which takes 231ms out of the 248ms total. With 25 instances, that's roughly 9ms per instance—this is where optimization would have the most impact.\n\nI'm also noticing that calculate_zero_hash runs 584 times sequentially with minimal per-call overhead, so batching these into a single kernel could reduce launch overhead significantly. The question is whether we can optimize the Poseidon2 hashing itself, though that likely lives in the Plonky3 crate or the CUDA backend's PCS implementation.\n\nLooking at the poseidon2_compressing_row_hashes_kernel with its 25 instances taking 231ms total, that's where the real bottleneck is. But since Poseidon2 hashing is fundamental to the PCS protocol, we're constrained in how much we can change it without altering the cryptographic scheme itself.\n\nShifting focus to MLE Rounds, there's about 5000 remaining calls to `main_ptrs.to_device()` that each trigger memory allocation and copy operations, contributing roughly 50-75ms of overhead. At 170ms total for this phase, eliminating that would be meaningful. The challenge is that batching these calls would require restructuring how `TraceCtx.main_ptrs_dev` is stored and propagated through the evaluator code, which creates a significant refactoring burden.\n\nLooking at WHIR next, it's taking 101ms at APC 300, with the algebraic batch kernel accounting for just 18ms across six instances. That means NTT and other operations are consuming the bulk of the time—around 82ms—making this a relatively constrained optimization target.\n\nFor GKR input evaluation, the prealloc report shows seg0 still spending roughly 256ms on this at APC 300, with the kernel execution dominating for large AIRs. The `evaluate_interactions_gkr_kernel` is accumulating 457ms of GPU time across 486 instances, and the largest individual runs hit 13.3ms. Improving the kernel's memory access patterns or leveraging shared memory more effectively could help, though that's a deeper kernel-level optimization.\n\nI'm also noticing `calculate_zero_hash` is burning 110ms across 584 instances—about 188 microseconds each. This looks like a sequential per-AIR loop that might benefit from batching into a single kernel or overlapping with other work, possibly as part of the Merkle tree construction for trace commitment.\n\nLet me step back and focus on the biggest remaining wins. The STARK excluding trace is sitting at 1407ms at APC 300, split between constraints (983ms: LogUp GKR at 522ms, Round 0 at 290ms, MLE rounds at 170ms) and openings (175ms, with the breakdown cut off).\n\nTo hit the 2x scaling target from APC 0, I need to get STARK excluding trace down to 1070ms at APC 300—that's roughly 337ms of reduction needed. The main candidates are LogUp GKR at 522ms (37% of the total, though algorithmic changes would be needed), Round 0 at 290ms (could improve with better kernel batching or parallelism), Trace Commit at 248ms (mostly hash-bound), and MLE rounds at 170ms (might benefit from batching main_ptrs).\n\nLet me think about this from a different angle—what actually scales with AIR count versus what doesn't.\n\nAt APC 0 there are 99 AIR instances across 5 segments, but at APC 300 that jumps to 623 AIRs across just 2 segments. The per-AIR costs like kernel launch overhead (5-10us), cudaMallocAsync (5-15us), and H2D copies add up quickly—with potentially 2000-5000 kernel launches across STARK excluding trace, that's 20-50ms just from launch overhead. The real problem is that many of those AIRs at APC 300 are tiny traces, so the per-AIR overhead per cell becomes prohibitively high. The GPU fundamentally struggles with thousands of small workloads.\n\nI should focus on identifying the single biggest optimization opportunity.\n\nLooking at the LogUp GKR phase, which accounts for 0.52s of the 0.98s constraint time at APC 300, I realize this includes both the GKR input evaluation and the tree proving itself. The tree proving phase—the fractional sumcheck GKR protocol—hasn't been optimized yet, even though the input evaluation already has multistream and preallocation. Let me dig into the detailed metrics to see where the time is actually going.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5894,"cache_read_input_tokens":51813,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5894},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50386b42-eb75-4189-9b73-2a1359832805","uuid":"154e26a9-f781-4c1d-b468-d7bb72d218d1"}
