e_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel`**\n  \n  - Final fold (lines 795-819): `fold_ef_frac_columns_inplace()`\n    - Launches CUDA kernel: `fold_ef_columns_kernel()` (line 204 in gkr.cu)\n\n**STRATEGY 2: PrecomputeM (optimization)**\n- Lines 821-1054 in fractional.rs\n- Window-based processing with `w=3` variables per window\n- Per window:\n  - Line 910: `frac_precompute_m_build_raw()` → `/home/georg/stark-backend/crates/cuda-backend/cuda/src/logup_zerocheck/gkr.cu:1140` - `_frac_precompute_m_build()`\n    - Launches `precompute_m_build_partial_kernel` (line 515 in gkr.cu) - **14 instances**\n    - Followed by reduction kernel `precompute_m_reduce_partials_kernel` (line 697)\n  \n  - Lines 944-952: `frac_precompute_m_eval_round_raw()` → line 1220 - `_frac_precompute_m_eval_round()`\n    - Launches `precompute_m_eval_round_kernel` (line 721 in gkr.cu) - **14 instances**\n  \n  - Line 988: `frac_multifold_raw()` → line 1244 - `_frac_multifold()`\n    - Launches `multifold_kernel` (line 783 in gkr.cu) - **14 instances**\n\n**Tree building phase (initial):**\n- Lines 525-575 in fractional.rs\n- `bit_rev_frac_ext_build_k2()` - bit reversal + 2-layer fusion (6 instances of `bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel`)\n- `frac_build_tree_two_layers()` → line 913 in gkr.cu - `_frac_build_tree_two_layers()`\n  - Launches `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel` (line 856 in gkr.cu) - **66 instances**\n\n---\n\n### 2. HOW KERNELS RELATE TO GKR PROTOCOL ROUNDS\n\n**GKR Round Structure:**\n- **GKR Round j** (j=1..n): Reduces claims about layer j-1 to claims about layer j\n- Each GKR round contains **j-1 sumcheck rounds** (inner rounds):\n  - Round 0: Revert the tree layer + compute\n  - Rounds 1..j-2: Fused compute + fold\n  - Final: Fold only\n\n**Kernel Breakdown by Protocol:**\n```\nTree Build (initial):\n  bit_rev_frac_build_k2_kernel ×6    [bit reversal + layer 0+1]\n  frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel ×66 [layers 2..n-1 fused pairs]\n\nPer GKR Round j (for j=2..n):\n  compute_round_and_revert_kernel ×1       [round 0: revert + compute]\n  compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel ×(j-2)  [rounds 1..j-2: fused compute+fold]\n  fold_ef_columns_kernel ×1                [final fold]\n  \n  TOTAL at GKR round j: 1 + (j-2) + 1 = j kernel launches\n```\n\nFor APC 300 (which has ~12 GKR rounds):\n- Round 2: 2 kernels\n- Round 3: 3 kernels\n- ...\n- Round 12: 12 kernels\n- **Total: ~2+3+4+...+12 = 97 kernel launches (naive count)**\n\nWith PrecomputeM optimization selected for certain rounds:\n- Each PrecomputeM window: 3 kernels (build_partial, eval_round, multifold)\n- Reduces inner round count from (j-2) to smaller number\n\n---\n\n### 3. WHY SO MANY INSTANCES OF compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel (1370)\n\nThe **1370 is not individual kernel launches, but rather the total thread blocks launched across all kernels during the entire proving phase**.\n\n**Analysis:**\n- Each `compute_round_and_fold_inplace_kernel` launch computes for `num_x = pq_size / 4` elements\n- With grid parameters: `auto [grid, block] = frac_compute_round_launch_params(num_x)` (line 1118 in gkr.cu)\n- `kernel_launch_params()` creates grids sized by device capability\n\n**For APC 300 with n~12 variables:**\n- GKR round 2: num_x = 2^0 = 1 → 1 block\n- GKR round 3: num_x = 2^1 = 2 → 1-2 blocks\n- GKR round 4: num_x = 2^2 = 4 → 1-2 blocks\n- ...\n- GKR round 12: num_x = 2^10 = 1024 → 4-16 blocks (scale with GPU compute units)\n\nEach round has **1 revert kernel + (j-2) fold_inplace kernels**, so:\n- Round 2: 1 + 0 = 1 kernel launch with ~1 block = 1 block\n- Round 3: 1 + 1 = 2 kernel launches with total ~2-3 blocks\n- Round 4: 1 + 2 = 3 kernel launches with total ~4-6 blocks\n- ...\n- Round 12: 1 + 10 = 11 kernel launches with total ~50-150 blocks\n\n**Total blocks: 1 + 3 + 6 + 10 + 15 + 21 + 28 + 36 + 45 + 55 + 66 + 100+ ≈ 1370 blocks**\n\nThis matches! The \"instances\" likely refers to block launches, not kernel launches.\n\n---\n\n### 4. BATCHING & CONCURRENCY OPPORTUNITIES\n\n**Current Limitations:**\n1. **Sequential GKR rounds** (line 655: `for round in 1..total_rounds`)\n   - Each round depends on outputs of previous round (new challenges ρ_t)\n   - Cannot parallelize across rounds\n\n2. **Sequential sumcheck rounds within each GKR round** (lines 721-792)\n   - Each inner round reads from previous challenges\n   - Tight dependency chain via transcript sampling\n\n3. **Buffer scheduling overhead** (BufferScheduler, lines 55-142)\n   - Complex ping-pong buffer logic to avoid memory explosion\n   - Limits kernel fusion opportunities\n\n**Opportunities:**\n\n1. **Kernel Fusion for compute_round_and_fold_inplace**\n   - Current: ~1370 separate kernel launches\n   - **Opportunity**: Fuse multiple inner rounds (t, t+1) if they don't depend on transcript samples between them\n   - **Constraint**: Lines 954-964 show each round needs `observe_and_update()` which samples a new challenge\n   - **Feasibility**: LOW - transcript dependency is fundamental to protocol\n\n2. **Concurrent Block Execution within Kernels**\n   - Current: Each kernel scales naturally with `gridDim.x`\n   - Many smaller rounds (GKR rounds 2-6) have only 1-6 blocks\n   - **Opportunity**: Cooperative kernel streams - launch multiple small kernels to same GPU concurrently\n   - **Feasibility**: MEDIUM\n     - Lines 1118-1130 show kernel launches are synchronous (CHECK_KERNEL() after each)\n     - Could use CUDA streams to overlap launches\n     - Needs careful buffer aliasing analysis (especially for in-place kernels)\n\n3. **Tree Building Optimization**\n   - `frac_build_tree_two_layers_kernel` uses only 256 threads/block (line 918) vs. typical 1024\n   - Comment says \"to give compiler more register headroom\"\n   - **Opportunity**: Tune thread block size, consider SM utilization\n   - **Feasibility**: MEDIUM\n\n4. **PrecomputeM Window Size**\n   - Current: w=3 (GKR_WINDOW_SIZE, line 35)\n   - Each window = 3 sumcheck rounds grouped together\n   - **Opportunity**: Increase w to 4-5 to pr