her 16ms.\n\nFor the openings phase, the stacked reduction contributes 74ms with the block sum kernel being the heaviest part, while WHIR takes up 101ms mostly from NTT/INTT operations at around 82ms.\n\nNow I'm refocusing on the stark-backend components and their optimization potential. Looking at the current breakdown at APC 300, LogUp GKR dominates at 522ms with the input evaluations already heavily optimized but tree proving still untouched, followed by Round 0 at 290ms and Trace Commit at 248ms. The MLE rounds and WHIR are relatively smaller targets, while stacked reduction has already seen significant gains from optimization work.\n\nFor the next steps, I'm considering batching the device uploads in MLE rounds to consolidate thousands of individual pointer transfers into a single batch per round, which could save 30-50ms with moderate complexity. I'm also looking at the GKR tree proving bottleneck where the sequential dependencies and kernel launch overhead from many small fold operations might be addressable through better batching or reduced launch overhead.\n\nMulti-stream MLE rounds could potentially save around 65ms if the GPU-bound portion is underutilized and we can achieve 2x parallelism, though this depends on actual GPU utilization patterns. For the trace commit hashing, the poseidon2 kernel is a major bottleneck at 231ms running on a single stream, and splitting this work across multiple streams could provide meaningful overlap with subsequent operations.\n\nThe Round 0 batching issue is interesting—those small batches of 20-42 AIRs never hit the multi-threading threshold of 100, so lowering that threshold or restructuring how batches are grouped could unlock parallelism gains of 20-50ms with relatively simple changes. I'm realizing the multi-threading operates at a different level than the batching—the prover likely processes all AIRs for a segment through a function that uses multi-threading internally, while batching splits AIRs into memory-const