{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01M1675TnhaChZehQG9G8hNq","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Plan: Batch MLE main_ptrs Upload\n2\t\n3\t## Goal\n4\t\n5\tEliminate per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` overhead in MLE sumcheck rounds by consolidating all per-round main_ptrs into a single flat vector and uploading it with one `to_device()` call. This removes ~5000 `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` pairs per benchmark, saving an estimated 30-50ms from the 170ms MLE Rounds span at APC 300.\n6\t\n7\t## Current Code Path\n8\t\n9\t### Entry point\n10\t\n11\t`LogupZerocheckGpu::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1111`\n12\t\n13\tThis function is called once per MLE round per segment. At APC 300 with 2 segments and ~8 rounds per segment, it executes ~16 times per benchmark.\n14\t\n15\t### Per-trace main_ptrs upload (the bottleneck)\n16\t\n17\t**Case A (late_eval, round > n_lift)** — lines 1188-1195:\n18\t```rust\n19\tlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..]\n20\t    .iter()\n21\t    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: m.buffer().as_ptr(), air_width: ... })\n22\t    .collect_vec();\n23\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // ← per-trace cudaMallocAsync + H2D\n24\t```\n25\t\n26\t**Case B (early_eval, round <= n_lift)** — lines 1335-1347:\n27\t```rust\n28\tlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..]\n29\t    .iter()\n30\t    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: base_ptr.wrapping_add(...), air_width: ... })\n31\t    .collect_vec();\n32\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // ← per-trace cudaMallocAsync + H2D\n33\t```\n34\t\n35\tEach call creates a `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` via:\n36\t1. `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(len)` → `cudaMallocAsync` (~5-10μs)\n37\t2. `copy_to()` → `cudaMemcpyAsync` H2D (~2-5μs)\n38\t\n39\tAt APC 300, ~310 traces per segment per round × ~8 rounds × 2 segments = ~5000 calls total. At ~10μs per call, this is ~50ms of pure overhead.\n40\t\n41\t### How main_ptrs_dev is consumed\n42\t\n43\t**TraceCtx struct** — `batch_mle.rs:89-105`:\n44\t```rust\n45\tpub(crate) struct TraceCtx {\n46\t    // ...\n47\t    pub main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>,  // owns the allocation\n48\t    // ...\n49\t}\n50\t```\n51\t\n52\tAll consumers only use the raw pointer:\n53\t1. **Batch builders** (`batch_mle.rs:188`, `batch_mle.rs:335`, `batch_mle_monomial.rs:184,407,672`): use `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()`\n54\t2. **Single-trace fallback** (`batch_mle.rs:473`): calls `evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu(&t.main_ptrs_dev)`, which internally uses `.as_ptr()` at `mle_round.rs:56`\n55\t3. **Single-trace fallback** (`batch_mle.rs:642`): calls `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu(&t.main_ptrs_dev)`, which internally uses `.as_ptr()` at `mle_round.rs:111`\n56\t\n57\tNeither fallback function uses `.len()` or any other `DeviceBuffer` method beyond `.as_ptr()`.\n58\t\n59\t## Changes\n60\t\n61\t### Change 1: Modify TraceCtx to use raw pointer\n62\t\n63\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:89-105`\n64\t\n65\tReplace:\n66\t```rust\n67\tpub main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>,\n68\t```\n69\tWith:\n70\t```rust\n71\tpub main_ptrs_ptr: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>,\n72\t```\n73\t\n74\t**Why**: TraceCtx no longer owns individual allocations. It holds a pointer into a shared device buffer that is kept alive by the caller.\n75\t\n76\t### Change 2: Update batch builder usage of main_ptrs\n77\t\n78\t**Files**:\n79\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:188` — `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new()`\n80\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:335` — `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new()`\n81\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:184` — `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::new()`\n82\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:407` — `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch::new()`\n83\t- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:672` — `LogupMonomialBatch::new()`\n84\t\n85\tChange `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr` at each site.\n86\t\n87\t**Why**: Trivial rename. The raw pointer is the same value that `.as_ptr()` returned.\n88\t\n89\t### Change 3: Update fallback evaluation function signatures\n90\t\n91\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mle_round.rs`\n92\t\n93\t`evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu()` (line 24): Change parameter from `d_main_ptrs: &DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to `d_main_ptrs: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>`. Update the call site at line 56 from `d_main_ptrs.as_ptr()` to `d_main_ptrs`.\n94\t\n95\t`evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu()` (line 78): Same change. Update call site at line 111.\n96\t\n97\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n98\t\n99\tUpdate call sites:\n100\t- Line 473: `&t.main_ptrs_dev` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr`\n101\t- Line 642: `&t.main_ptrs_dev` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr`\n102\t\n103\t**Why**: These functions only need the raw pointer. Removing the `DeviceBuffer` reference requirement allows them to work with shared-buffer pointers.\n104\t\n105\t### Change 4: Batch main_ptrs upload in sumcheck_polys_batch_eval\n106\t\n107\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n108\t\n109\t#### 4a: Batch Case A (late_eval) main_ptrs\n110\t\n111\tAfter the Case A loop (current lines 1173-1211), instead of calling `main_ptrs.to_device()` per trace:\n112\t\n113\t1. Collect all Case A main_ptrs into a flat `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets.\n114\t2. After the loop, do a single `all_late_main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n115\t3. Each `late_eval` TraceCtx gets `main_ptrs_ptr` pointing into the shared buffer at its offset.\n116\t4. Store the shared buffer as `_keepalive_late_main_ptrs` to keep the device memory alive.\n117\t\n118\t```rust\n119\t// Before Case A loop:\n120\tlet mut all_late_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::new();\n121\tlet mut late_main_ptrs_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\n122\t\n123\t// Inside Case A (round == n_lift + 1):\n124\tlate_main_ptrs_offsets.push(all_late_main_ptrs.len());\n125\tlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..].iter()\n126\t    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: m.buffer().as_ptr(), air_width: ... })\n127\t    .collect_vec();\n128\tall_late_main_ptrs.extend(main_ptrs);\n129\t// Don't call to_device() yet — defer to after the loop\n130\t\n131\t// After Case A loop:\n132\tlet _keepalive_late_main_ptrs = if !all_late_main_ptrs.is_empty() {\n133\t    let buf = all_late_main_ptrs.to_device()?;\n134\t    // Backfill pointers into late_eval TraceCtx entries\n135\t    for (i, t) in late_eval.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n136\t        t.main_ptrs_ptr = unsafe { buf.as_ptr().add(late_main_ptrs_offsets[i]) };\n137\t    }\n138\t    Some(buf)\n139\t} else {\n140\t    None\n141\t};\n142\t```\n143\t\n144\t**Implementation note**: The Case A loop currently pushes to `late_eval` inside the loop. We need to restructure slightly: defer `late_eval.push()` until after the device upload, or push with a placeholder pointer and backfill. The backfill approach is simpler — push with `std::ptr::null()` initially, then fill in after upload.\n145\t\n146\t#### 4b: Batch Case B (early_eval) main_ptrs\n147\t\n148\tAfter Phase 3 (current lines 1303-1364), instead of calling `main_ptrs.to_device()` per trace:\n149\t\n150\t1. Extend the Phase 3 loop to collect all Case B main_ptrs into a flat `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets.\n151\t2. After the loop, do a single `all_early_main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n152\t3. Each `early_eval` TraceCtx gets `main_ptrs_ptr` pointing into the shared buffer at its offset.\n153\t4. Store the shared buffer as `_keepalive_early_main_ptrs`.\n154\t\n155\t```rust\n156\t// Before Phase 3 loop:\n157\tlet mut all_early_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::new();\n158\tlet mut early_main_ptrs_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\n159\t\n160\t// Inside Phase 3 per-trace loop:\n161\tearly_main_ptrs_offsets.push(all_early_main_ptrs.len());\n162\tlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..].iter()\n163\t    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: base_ptr.wrapping_add(...), air_width: ... })\n164\t    .collect_vec();\n165\tall_early_main_ptrs.extend(main_ptrs);\n166\t\n167\t// After Phase 3 loop:\n168\tlet _keepalive_early_main_ptrs = if !all_early_main_ptrs.is_empty() {\n169\t    let buf = all_early_main_ptrs.to_device()?;\n170\t    for (i, t) in early_eval.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n171\t        t.main_ptrs_ptr = unsafe { buf.as_ptr().add(early_main_ptrs_offsets[i]) };\n172\t    }\n173\t    Some(buf)\n174\t} else {\n175\t    None\n176\t};\n177\t```\n178\t\n179\t**Why**: This is the main optimization. ~310 Case B traces per round → 1 upload. The total data is ~310 × 2 entries × 16 bytes = ~10KB per round — a trivial single upload.\n180\t\n181\t### Change 5: Update test file\n182\t\n183\t**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/tests.rs:707-726`\n184\t\n185\tChange:\n186\t```rust\n187\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device().unwrap();\n188\t// ...\n189\tmain_ptrs_dev,\n190\t```\n191\tTo:\n192\t```rust\n193\tlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device().unwrap();\n194\t// ...\n195\tmain_ptrs_ptr: main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr(),\n196\t```\n197\t\n198\tAdd `_keepalive` binding to keep `main_ptrs_dev` alive for the test scope.\n199\t\n200\t**Why**: Test constructs TraceCtx directly and needs to match the new field name.\n201\t\n202\t## Invariants\n203\t\n204\t1. **Pointer validity**: Every `main_ptrs_ptr` in a TraceCtx must point into a device buffer that outlives all uses of that TraceCtx. The `_keepalive_*` variables in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` ensure this — they are declared before the evaluation calls and dropped at function exit.\n205\t\n206\t2. **Pointer alignment**: `MainMatrixPtrs<EF>` is `#[repr(C)]` with natural alignment. Pointers into a flat `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` are correctly aligned since the buffer is a contiguous array.\n207\t\n208\t3. **Correctness**: The raw pointer values are identical to what `.as_ptr()` returned from the individual `DeviceBuffer`s. The data content (matrix pointers + widths) is unchanged. GPU kernels receive the same pointer values.\n209\t\n210\t4. **No behavioral change for APC 0**: At APC 0, the multi-threading threshold is not met and there are few traces (~20 per segment). The batching still works but produces a small flat vector. No regression expected.\n211\t\n212\t5. **Fallback path correctness**: The fallback evaluation functions (`evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu`, `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu`) receive the same pointer value they received before. The only difference is the function signature changes from `&DeviceBuffer<T>` to `*const T`.\n213\t\n214\t## Measurement Plan\n215\t\n216\tRun the standard benchmark:\n217\t```bash\n218\tcd /home/georg/powdr && openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n219\t```\n220\t\n221\tAnalyze with spec.py:\n222\t```bash\n223\tpython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json apc300\n224\tpython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc100/metrics.json apc100\n225\tpython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json apc000\n226\t```\n227\t\n228\t### Success criteria\n229\t\n230\t| Metric | APC 300 Target | Rationale |\n231\t|--------|---------------|-----------|\n232\t| MLE Rounds | < 145ms | ≥15% improvement from 170ms |\n233\t| STARK excl trace | < 1390ms | ≥17ms net improvement |\n234\t| STARK excl trace APC 0 | < 2205ms | No regression (< 3% increase from 2140ms) |\n235\t\n236\t### Verification\n237\t\n238\t1. Prove + verify passes for all 3 APC configs (0, 100, 300)\n239\t2. Run APC 300 three times to confirm consistency (MLE Rounds std < 10ms)\n240\t\n241\t## Rollback Criteria\n242\t\n243\tRevert if ANY of:\n244\t1. MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms (below measurement noise)\n245\t2. STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by > 65ms\n246\t3. Prove + verify fails for any APC config\n247\t4. GPU OOM at any APC config\n248\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"50386b42-eb75-4189-9b73-2a1359832805","uuid":"ac34592f-8db8-4501-a4af-05d83331ae49","timestamp":"2026-04-13T09:47:48.986Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/georg/stark-backend/autoopt-results/2026-04-13-1430-batch-mle-main-ptrs-upload/plan.md","content":"# Plan: Batch MLE main_ptrs Upload\n\n## Goal\n\nEliminate per-trace `main_ptrs.to_device()` overhead in MLE sumcheck rounds by consolidating all per-round main_ptrs into a single flat vector and uploading it with one `to_device()` call. This removes ~5000 `cudaMallocAsync` + `cudaMemcpyAsync` pairs per benchmark, saving an estimated 30-50ms from the 170ms MLE Rounds span at APC 300.\n\n## Current Code Path\n\n### Entry point\n\n`LogupZerocheckGpu::sumcheck_polys_batch_eval()` at `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs:1111`\n\nThis function is called once per MLE round per segment. At APC 300 with 2 segments and ~8 rounds per segment, it executes ~16 times per benchmark.\n\n### Per-trace main_ptrs upload (the bottleneck)\n\n**Case A (late_eval, round > n_lift)** — lines 1188-1195:\n```rust\nlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..]\n    .iter()\n    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: m.buffer().as_ptr(), air_width: ... })\n    .collect_vec();\nlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // ← per-trace cudaMallocAsync + H2D\n```\n\n**Case B (early_eval, round <= n_lift)** — lines 1335-1347:\n```rust\nlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..]\n    .iter()\n    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: base_ptr.wrapping_add(...), air_width: ... })\n    .collect_vec();\nlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device()?;  // ← per-trace cudaMallocAsync + H2D\n```\n\nEach call creates a `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` via:\n1. `DeviceBuffer::with_capacity(len)` → `cudaMallocAsync` (~5-10μs)\n2. `copy_to()` → `cudaMemcpyAsync` H2D (~2-5μs)\n\nAt APC 300, ~310 traces per segment per round × ~8 rounds × 2 segments = ~5000 calls total. At ~10μs per call, this is ~50ms of pure overhead.\n\n### How main_ptrs_dev is consumed\n\n**TraceCtx struct** — `batch_mle.rs:89-105`:\n```rust\npub(crate) struct TraceCtx {\n    // ...\n    pub main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>,  // owns the allocation\n    // ...\n}\n```\n\nAll consumers only use the raw pointer:\n1. **Batch builders** (`batch_mle.rs:188`, `batch_mle.rs:335`, `batch_mle_monomial.rs:184,407,672`): use `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()`\n2. **Single-trace fallback** (`batch_mle.rs:473`): calls `evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu(&t.main_ptrs_dev)`, which internally uses `.as_ptr()` at `mle_round.rs:56`\n3. **Single-trace fallback** (`batch_mle.rs:642`): calls `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu(&t.main_ptrs_dev)`, which internally uses `.as_ptr()` at `mle_round.rs:111`\n\nNeither fallback function uses `.len()` or any other `DeviceBuffer` method beyond `.as_ptr()`.\n\n## Changes\n\n### Change 1: Modify TraceCtx to use raw pointer\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:89-105`\n\nReplace:\n```rust\npub main_ptrs_dev: DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>,\n```\nWith:\n```rust\npub main_ptrs_ptr: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>,\n```\n\n**Why**: TraceCtx no longer owns individual allocations. It holds a pointer into a shared device buffer that is kept alive by the caller.\n\n### Change 2: Update batch builder usage of main_ptrs\n\n**Files**:\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:188` — `ZerocheckMleBatchBuilder::new()`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs:335` — `LogupMleBatchBuilder::new()`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:184` — `ZerocheckMonomialBatch::new()`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:407` — `ZerocheckMonomialParYBatch::new()`\n- `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle_monomial.rs:672` — `LogupMonomialBatch::new()`\n\nChange `t.main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr()` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr` at each site.\n\n**Why**: Trivial rename. The raw pointer is the same value that `.as_ptr()` returned.\n\n### Change 3: Update fallback evaluation function signatures\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mle_round.rs`\n\n`evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu()` (line 24): Change parameter from `d_main_ptrs: &DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` to `d_main_ptrs: *const MainMatrixPtrs<EF>`. Update the call site at line 56 from `d_main_ptrs.as_ptr()` to `d_main_ptrs`.\n\n`evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu()` (line 78): Same change. Update call site at line 111.\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/batch_mle.rs`\n\nUpdate call sites:\n- Line 473: `&t.main_ptrs_dev` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr`\n- Line 642: `&t.main_ptrs_dev` → `t.main_ptrs_ptr`\n\n**Why**: These functions only need the raw pointer. Removing the `DeviceBuffer` reference requirement allows them to work with shared-buffer pointers.\n\n### Change 4: Batch main_ptrs upload in sumcheck_polys_batch_eval\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/logup_zerocheck/mod.rs`\n\n#### 4a: Batch Case A (late_eval) main_ptrs\n\nAfter the Case A loop (current lines 1173-1211), instead of calling `main_ptrs.to_device()` per trace:\n\n1. Collect all Case A main_ptrs into a flat `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets.\n2. After the loop, do a single `all_late_main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n3. Each `late_eval` TraceCtx gets `main_ptrs_ptr` pointing into the shared buffer at its offset.\n4. Store the shared buffer as `_keepalive_late_main_ptrs` to keep the device memory alive.\n\n```rust\n// Before Case A loop:\nlet mut all_late_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::new();\nlet mut late_main_ptrs_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\n\n// Inside Case A (round == n_lift + 1):\nlate_main_ptrs_offsets.push(all_late_main_ptrs.len());\nlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..].iter()\n    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: m.buffer().as_ptr(), air_width: ... })\n    .collect_vec();\nall_late_main_ptrs.extend(main_ptrs);\n// Don't call to_device() yet — defer to after the loop\n\n// After Case A loop:\nlet _keepalive_late_main_ptrs = if !all_late_main_ptrs.is_empty() {\n    let buf = all_late_main_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    // Backfill pointers into late_eval TraceCtx entries\n    for (i, t) in late_eval.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n        t.main_ptrs_ptr = unsafe { buf.as_ptr().add(late_main_ptrs_offsets[i]) };\n    }\n    Some(buf)\n} else {\n    None\n};\n```\n\n**Implementation note**: The Case A loop currently pushes to `late_eval` inside the loop. We need to restructure slightly: defer `late_eval.push()` until after the device upload, or push with a placeholder pointer and backfill. The backfill approach is simpler — push with `std::ptr::null()` initially, then fill in after upload.\n\n#### 4b: Batch Case B (early_eval) main_ptrs\n\nAfter Phase 3 (current lines 1303-1364), instead of calling `main_ptrs.to_device()` per trace:\n\n1. Extend the Phase 3 loop to collect all Case B main_ptrs into a flat `Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` with per-trace offsets.\n2. After the loop, do a single `all_early_main_ptrs.to_device()`.\n3. Each `early_eval` TraceCtx gets `main_ptrs_ptr` pointing into the shared buffer at its offset.\n4. Store the shared buffer as `_keepalive_early_main_ptrs`.\n\n```rust\n// Before Phase 3 loop:\nlet mut all_early_main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = Vec::new();\nlet mut early_main_ptrs_offsets: Vec<usize> = Vec::new();\n\n// Inside Phase 3 per-trace loop:\nearly_main_ptrs_offsets.push(all_early_main_ptrs.len());\nlet main_ptrs: Vec<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>> = mats[first_main_idx..].iter()\n    .map(|m| MainMatrixPtrs { data: base_ptr.wrapping_add(...), air_width: ... })\n    .collect_vec();\nall_early_main_ptrs.extend(main_ptrs);\n\n// After Phase 3 loop:\nlet _keepalive_early_main_ptrs = if !all_early_main_ptrs.is_empty() {\n    let buf = all_early_main_ptrs.to_device()?;\n    for (i, t) in early_eval.iter_mut().enumerate() {\n        t.main_ptrs_ptr = unsafe { buf.as_ptr().add(early_main_ptrs_offsets[i]) };\n    }\n    Some(buf)\n} else {\n    None\n};\n```\n\n**Why**: This is the main optimization. ~310 Case B traces per round → 1 upload. The total data is ~310 × 2 entries × 16 bytes = ~10KB per round — a trivial single upload.\n\n### Change 5: Update test file\n\n**File**: `crates/cuda-backend/src/tests.rs:707-726`\n\nChange:\n```rust\nlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device().unwrap();\n// ...\nmain_ptrs_dev,\n```\nTo:\n```rust\nlet main_ptrs_dev = main_ptrs.to_device().unwrap();\n// ...\nmain_ptrs_ptr: main_ptrs_dev.as_ptr(),\n```\n\nAdd `_keepalive` binding to keep `main_ptrs_dev` alive for the test scope.\n\n**Why**: Test constructs TraceCtx directly and needs to match the new field name.\n\n## Invariants\n\n1. **Pointer validity**: Every `main_ptrs_ptr` in a TraceCtx must point into a device buffer that outlives all uses of that TraceCtx. The `_keepalive_*` variables in `sumcheck_polys_batch_eval` ensure this — they are declared before the evaluation calls and dropped at function exit.\n\n2. **Pointer alignment**: `MainMatrixPtrs<EF>` is `#[repr(C)]` with natural alignment. Pointers into a flat `DeviceBuffer<MainMatrixPtrs<EF>>` are correctly aligned since the buffer is a contiguous array.\n\n3. **Correctness**: The raw pointer values are identical to what `.as_ptr()` returned from the individual `DeviceBuffer`s. The data content (matrix pointers + widths) is unchanged. GPU kernels receive the same pointer values.\n\n4. **No behavioral change for APC 0**: At APC 0, the multi-threading threshold is not met and there are few traces (~20 per segment). The batching still works but produces a small flat vector. No regression expected.\n\n5. **Fallback path correctness**: The fallback evaluation functions (`evaluate_mle_constraints_gpu`, `evaluate_mle_interactions_gpu`) receive the same pointer value they received before. The only difference is the function signature changes from `&DeviceBuffer<T>` to `*const T`.\n\n## Measurement Plan\n\nRun the standard benchmark:\n```bash\ncd /home/georg/powdr && openvm-riscv/scripts/run_pairing.sh\n```\n\nAnalyze with spec.py:\n```bash\npython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc300/metrics.json apc300\npython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc100/metrics.json apc100\npython /home/georg/spec.py results/pairing/apc000/metrics.json apc000\n```\n\n### Success criteria\n\n| Metric | APC 300 Target | Rationale |\n|--------|---------------|-----------|\n| MLE Rounds | < 145ms | ≥15% improvement from 170ms |\n| STARK excl trace | < 1390ms | ≥17ms net improvement |\n| STARK excl trace APC 0 | < 2205ms | No regression (< 3% increase from 2140ms) |\n\n### Verification\n\n1. Prove + verify passes for all 3 APC configs (0, 100, 300)\n2. Run APC 300 three times to confirm consistency (MLE Rounds std < 10ms)\n\n## Rollback Criteria\n\nRevert if ANY of:\n1. MLE Rounds at APC 300 improves by < 10ms (below measurement noise)\n2. STARK excl trace at APC 0 regresses by > 65ms\n3. Prove + verify fails for any APC config\n4. GPU OOM at any APC config\n","numLines":248,"startLine":1,"totalLines":248}}}
